<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=electric-motor">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Electric motor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An electric motor is a machine that converts electrical energy into mechanical energy, usually through the interaction of a magnetic field and the current in a wire winding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a> first showed electromagnetic rotation on 3 September 1821 at the Royal Institution, where a wire carrying a current turned around a magnet in a pool of mercury. Ányos Jedlik built a device with a stator, rotor and commutator in 1828, William Sturgeon a DC motor able to turn machinery in 1832, and Frank Julian Sprague the first practical DC motor in 1886. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a> Ferraris and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-tesla">Nikola Tesla</a> invented AC induction motors in 1885 and 1887.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/electric-motor.jpg" alt="Electric motor" width="330" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zureks · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stator_and_rotor_by_Zureks.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before electromagnetic motors, experimenters investigated motors that worked by electrostatic force. The first were simple devices described in the 1740s and 1750s by the Scottish monk Andrew Gordon and the American Benjamin Franklin. Their theoretical principle, Coulomb's law, was discovered but not published by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-cavendish">Henry Cavendish</a> in 1771 and independently by Charles-Augustin de Coulomb in 1785. The high voltages needed were hard to generate, and electrostatic motors were never put to practical use.</p><p>William Sturgeon invented the first commutator direct-current motor able to turn machinery in 1832. Thomas Davenport and Emily Davenport then built a motor which he patented in 1837, running at up to 600 revolutions per minute and powering machine tools and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press. Primary batteries were so costly that it was commercially unsuccessful and bankrupted them, and with no electricity distribution system there was no practical market. In 1879 Walter Baily demonstrated Arago's rotating magnetic fields, by manually turning switches on and off, as in effect the first primitive induction motor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>An electric motor turns electrical energy into mechanical energy, most often through the interaction of a magnetic field and the current in a wire winding, which produces torque on the shaft. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-generator">electric generator</a> is mechanically identical but works in reverse. A motor has two mechanical parts, the rotor, which moves, and the stator, which does not. The stator core is built of thin insulated metal sheets called laminations, which reduce the losses from eddy currents. In a commutated motor a commutator reverses the current in the rotor windings every half turn, so that the torque always acts in the same direction; without it the rotor would stop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Standardised motors provide power for industry. The largest, used for marine propulsion, pipeline compression and pumped storage, exceed 100 megawatts. Other uses are fans, blowers and pumps, machine tools, household appliances, power tools, vehicles and disk drives, and small motors are found in electric watches. In regenerative braking with traction motors, a motor can be used in reverse as a generator to recover energy otherwise lost as heat and friction. Since the 1980s the market share of direct-current motors has declined in favour of alternating-current motors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Electric motor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1821</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Royal Institution</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/michael-faraday">Michael Faraday</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Andrew Gordon and Benjamin Franklin described electrostatic motors in the 1740s and 1750s, but these were never put to practical use; Faraday's device of 1821 was the first electromagnetic one.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">Electric battery<span>1800</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=electric-motor">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
